      "label": "(a)(i)",
      "part_stem": "A ball Y moves along a horizontal frictionless surface and collides with a ball Z, as illustrated in the views from above in Fig. 4.1 and Fig. 4.2. Fig. 4.1 (not to scale) shows BEFORE COLLISION with ball Y of mass $0.25\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ moving along line PQ towards ball Z of mass $m_{Z}$ which is initially stationary. Fig. 4.2 (not to scale) shows AFTER COLLISION with ball Y of mass $0.25\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ with velocity $3.7\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ at an angle of $27^{\\circ}$ to line PQ and ball Z of mass $m_{Z}$ with velocity $5.5\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ at an angle of $44^{\\circ}$ to line PQ on the opposite side of line PQ. Ball Y has a mass of $0.25\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ and initially moves along a line PQ. Ball Z has a mass $m_{Z}$ and is initially stationary. After the collision, ball Y has a final velocity of $3.7\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ at an angle of $27^{\\circ}$ to line PQ and ball Z has a final velocity of $5.5\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ at an angle of $44^{\\circ}$ to line PQ.",
      "text": "Calculate the component of the final momentum of ball Y in the direction perpendicular to line PQ.",
      "marks": 2,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(a)(ii)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "By considering the component of the final momentum of each ball in the direction perpendicular to line PQ, calculate $m_{Z}$.",
      "marks": 1,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(a)(iii)",
      "part_stem": null,
      "text": "During the collision, the average force exerted on Y by Z is $F_{Y}$ and the average force exerted on Z by Y is $F_{Z}$. Compare the magnitudes and directions of $F_{Y}$ and $F_{Z}$. Numerical values are not required.",
      "marks": 2,
      "answer_prompt": null,
      "unit": null
    },
    {
      "label": "(b)",
      "part_stem": "Two blocks, A and B, move directly towards each other along a horizontal frictionless surface, as shown in the view from above in Fig. 4.3. Fig. 4.3 shows block A moving with $4\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ to the right and block B moving with $6\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ to the left. The blocks collide perfectly elastically. Before the collision, block A has a speed of $4\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and block B has a speed of $6\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. After the collision, block B moves back along its original path with a speed of $2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.",
      "text": "Calculate the speed of block A after the collision.",
